<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="broker-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="broker-title">If you are a broker</h2>
      <p>Keep one register of every deal, showing:</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Property and parties, at a level you can defend</li>
        <li>Commission agreed, and any sub-broker's share</li>
        <li>Invoice raised, and GST if applicable</li>
        <li>TDS deducted, and whether it appears on your statement</li>
        <li>Date and mode of receipt</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">If you share commission with another broker, record it properly, as TDS and GST questions may follow. Avoid cash deals, as they create risk for you and your clients. The presumptive scheme for businesses in section 58 of the Income-tax Act, 2025 excludes those earning commission or brokerage, so plan on keeping proper accounts.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>If you are a developer</h2>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Keep a separate cost ledger for each project: land, approvals, construction, finance and marketing</li>
        <li>Keep a customer ledger for each unit: agreement, payment schedule, receipts and TDS</li>
        <li>Know your RERA position. Under section 4(2)(l)(D), as explained by commentators, 70% of amounts realised from allottees is to go to a separate account, with withdrawals tied to progress and certified</li>
        <li>Decide how revenue is recognised, with a professional, because it affects reported profit</li>
        <li>Check the input conditions before relying on a GST rate</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="buy-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="buy-title" class="first-h2">TDS on property transactions</h2>
      <p>This applies widely to buyers, and brokers are often asked about it. Under section 393(1) of the Income-tax Act, 2025, tax at 1% of the consideration or stamp duty value, whichever is higher, applies to transfers of immovable property other than agricultural land where that amount is ₹50 lakh or more. Secondary sources say Form 141 replaces Form 26QB from 1 April 2026. Non-resident sellers follow different rules, so get advice in that case.</p>
      <p>Do not advise clients on this yourself unless you are qualified. Point them to the {% ext "https://www.incometax.gov.in/", "income tax portal" %} or a professional.</p>

      <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Brokers treating all receipts as commission,</strong> including token money held for a client.</li>
        <li><strong>Developers pooling cash across projects,</strong> which breaks RERA and hides each project's profit.</li>
        <li><strong>Unit cancellations with no proper record,</strong> which complicate tax and GST.</li>
        <li><strong>Using a joint development deal without tax advice.</strong> These have specific treatment.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="ask">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Do you work with brokers or developers? They are different specialisms.</li>
        <li>For developers: how do you handle project accounting and RERA certification?</li>
        <li>Who is qualified to certify what? Certification for RERA may need a specific professional.</li>
        <li>For brokers: how will you match each deal with TDS?</li>
      </ul>

